Important difference

Probe	           Purpose	                                    If it fails
livenessProbe     	Is the container alive?	                 Restart container
readinessProbe	   Can the container receive traffic?         	Remove Pod from Service endpoints
startupProbe	Has the slow-starting application finished starting?	Prevent liveness/readiness checks until startup succeeds

So remember:
Liveness = restart
Readiness = traffic
Startup = starting

-------------------------------------------------
livenessProbe in Kubernetes
----------------
livenessProbe is used to check whether a container is still alive/healthy.

If the liveness probe fails repeatedly, Kubernetes assumes the container is stuck and restarts the container.

Example
containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:latest

    livenessProbe:
      httpGet:
        path: /
        port: 80
      initialDelaySeconds: 10
      periodSeconds: 5

What each part means
livenessProbe:     ➡️ Tell Kubernetes: "Check whether this container is alive."
httpGet:           ➡️ Kubernetes sends an HTTP request to the container.
path: /            ➡️ It checks: http://<pod-ip>:80/
port: 80           ➡️ The application must be listening on port 80.
initialDelaySeconds: 10  ➡️ Wait 10 seconds after the container starts before beginning the checks.
periodSeconds: 5    ➡️ Check every 5 seconds.

If the check fails
For example:

Container starts
      ↓
Wait 10 seconds
      ↓
Check /
      ↓
   Success → Container continues
      ↓
   Failure
      ↓
Repeated failures
      ↓
Kubernetes restarts container

Interview answer:  "livenessProbe checks whether a container is alive and functioning. If the probe fails repeatedly, Kubernetes restarts the container."

----------------------------------------------------
The easiest way is to understand what problem each probe solves.

1. First understand the situation
Suppose we have an application Pod:

                Application Pod
              ┌─────────────────┐
              │   Container     │
              │                 │
Client ──────►│   Application   │
              │                 │
              └─────────────────┘

The application can be in different states:

State A — Application is working
Application
    ↓
Working normally
    ↓
Can receive requests

Everything is fine.

State B — Application process is stuck

The container is still running, but the application is not responding.

Container = RUNNING
Application = STUCK

Kubernetes needs to know:  "Is this container actually alive, or is it stuck?"
That's what livenessProbe is for.


2. livenessProbe
Meaning
Liveness = "Is my application still alive?"

Example:
livenessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /health
    port: 8080

Kubernetes periodically checks:  http://Pod-IP:8080/health
Suppose the application responds:  HTTP 200 OK

Kubernetes thinks:  Application is alive ✅

But suppose it repeatedly fails:
HTTP request
    ↓
No response / error
    ↓
Probe fails
    ↓
Repeated failures
    ↓
Kubernetes restarts the container
Why restart?

Because the application may be stuck.
For example:

Container
   ↓
Java application
   ↓
Deadlock / stuck
   ↓
Application doesn't respond

Restarting may fix the problem.

Important
A liveness failure does not normally mean Kubernetes deletes the Pod.
It restarts the container inside the Pod.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. readinessProbe

Now imagine a different problem.
The application is alive:
Container = running ✅
Application = alive ✅

But it is temporarily not ready to receive user traffic.

For example, your application is starting:
Application started
      ↓
Loading data from database
      ↓
Loading configuration
      ↓
Not ready yet

The application is alive, but sending user requests to it may cause errors.
That's what readinessProbe checks.


Meaning
Readiness = "Can I send traffic to this Pod?"

Example:
readinessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /ready
    port: 8080

Kubernetes checks:  http://Pod-IP:8080/ready

If successful:
Pod READY ✅
    ↓
Service can send traffic

If it fails:
Pod NOT READY ❌
    ↓
Service stops sending traffic to this Pod

Very important
Readiness failure does NOT restart the container.
It simply says:  "Don't send traffic to me right now."



Connect this with Service
Suppose you have:
                 Service
                    |
        ┌───────────┼───────────┐
        ↓           ↓           ↓
      Pod A       Pod B       Pod C
      Ready       Ready      Not Ready

The Service has three Pods as possible endpoints.

But Pod C fails its readiness probe:
Pod C
  ↓
readinessProbe fails
  ↓
Not Ready
  ↓
Service removes Pod C from usable endpoints

Now traffic goes:

                 Service
                 /      \
                ↓        ↓
             Pod A     Pod B
             Ready     Ready

             Pod C
           Not Ready ❌

Pod C is still running.
It is just not receiving Service traffic.






Imagine 2 Pods behind a Service
             Service
                |
        ┌───────┴───────┐
        ↓               ↓
      Pod A           Pod B
      Ready           Ready

The Service sends traffic to both:

User
 ↓
Service
 ↓       ↓
Pod A   Pod B

Now Kubernetes is checking Pod A's readiness probe every 5 seconds.

10:00:00 → check Pod A → ✅
10:00:05 → check Pod A → ✅
10:00:10 → check Pod A → ❌

When the check fails, Kubernetes changes:

Pod A
Ready → NotReady

Now:

             Service
                |
                ↓
              Pod B
              Ready

              Pod A
            NotReady ❌

So new traffic goes:

User
 ↓
Service
 ↓
Pod B

Pod A is still running! It is just not receiving traffic from that Service.

How can YOU see this?
Run:  kubectl get pods

You might see:
NAME     READY   STATUS
pod-a    0/1     Running
pod-b    1/1     Running

This means:
pod-a
0/1 → Not Ready
Running → Container is still running

And:

pod-b
1/1 → Ready
Running → Container is running
The main thing to remember
Running ≠ Ready

A Pod can be:

Running + Ready       → Service sends traffic ✅
Running + NotReady    → Service does not send traffic ❌

The readiness probe is what tells Kubernetes:

"This Pod is ready to receive traffic" or "Don't send traffic to this Pod right now."



It will continue checking every ~5 seconds even after a failure.

Example:

readinessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /health
    port: 8080
  periodSeconds: 5

Imagine:

10:00:00 → /health → ✅ Healthy → Ready
10:00:05 → /health → ✅ Healthy → Ready
10:00:10 → /health → ❌ Failed  → NotReady
10:00:15 → /health → ❌ Failed  → NotReady
10:00:20 → /health → ❌ Failed  → NotReady
10:00:25 → /health → ✅ Healthy → Ready again

So failure does not stop the checking.

It keeps checking:

        every ~5 seconds
              ↓
       ┌─────────────┐
       │ readiness   │
       │   check     │
       └──────┬──────┘
              ↓
       ┌──────┴──────┐
       ↓             ↓
     Success       Failure
       ↓             ↓
     Ready        NotReady
       │             │
       └──────┬──────┘
              ↓
       wait ~5 seconds
              ↓
        CHECK AGAIN
Why?

Because the application might recover.

For example:

10:10 → ❌ database unavailable
       → Pod becomes NotReady

10:15 → database works again
       → /health → ✅
       → Pod becomes Ready

If Kubernetes stopped checking after the first failure, it would never know that the Pod recovered.

So remember:

Readiness probe keeps checking continuously while the Pod exists. Success = Ready. Failure = NotReady. Then it checks again.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------


5. startupProbe
Now consider a slow application.

For example, a Java application might need:
Container starts
      ↓
Java starts
      ↓
Load configuration
      ↓
Connect to database
      ↓
Load cache
      ↓
Application starts
It might take 60 seconds.


If you immediately use livenessProbe:
livenessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /health
    port: 8080
  periodSeconds: 5

Kubernetes might check too early:
0 sec → application starting
5 sec → probe fails
10 sec → probe fails
15 sec → probe fails
...

Kubernetes may conclude:  "Application is unhealthy."
and restart it.

But the application wasn't actually broken.
It was simply slow to start.

That's why we have:  startupProbe
It means:  "Give my application time to start."

Example:
startupProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /health
    port: 8080
  failureThreshold: 30
  periodSeconds: 5

This allows:  30 × 5 seconds = 150 seconds
for startup.


Your application needs 180 seconds
0 sec
 ↓
Container starts
 ↓
Startup probe keeps checking
 ↓
5 sec  ❌
10 sec ❌
15 sec ❌
...
 ↓
150 sec ❌  ← failureThreshold reached
 ↓
Kubernetes says:
"Application didn't start in time"
 ↓
Container is restarted 🔄

The problem is:

Allowed startup time = 150 sec
Application startup time = 180 sec

180 > 150
So the application doesn't get enough time to start.



Think of it like this:
Maximum allowed startup period ≈ 150 seconds
                 │
                 ▼
0s     → check ❌
5s     → check ❌
10s    → check ❌
15s    → check ❌
20s    → check ❌
...
50s    → check ❌
...
100s   → check ❌
...
140s   → check ❌
150s   → check ❌
                 │
                 ▼
        30 failures reached
                 │
                 ▼
       Container restarted

But if your application becomes ready at 80 seconds:

0s   → ❌
5s   → ❌
10s  → ❌
...
75s  → ❌
80s  → ✅
       │
       ▼
Startup probe succeeds
       │
       ▼
Startup probing stops
       │
       ▼
Liveness/readiness probes can take over





6. How startupProbe works with livenessProbe
This is the important part.

Suppose:
startupProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /health
    port: 8080
  periodSeconds: 5
  failureThreshold: 30

livenessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /health
    port: 8080
  periodSeconds: 5

When the container starts:
Container starts
      ↓
Startup probe runs
      ↓
Is application started?
      ↓
      NO
      ↓
Keep waiting
      ↓
Startup probe runs again
      ↓
      NO
      ↓
Keep waiting

Eventually:
Application finishes starting
          ↓
Startup probe succeeds ✅
          ↓
Startup phase finished
          ↓
Liveness probe starts checking

So:
STARTUP
   ↓
startupProbe
   ↓
Successful
   ↓
LIVENESS
   ↓
livenessProbe
   ↓
Healthy → continue
Unhealthy → restart



7. All three together

This is the easiest diagram to remember:

                 Container starts
                       │
                       ▼
               ┌─────────────────┐
               │ startupProbe     │
               │                 │
               │ "Has it started?"│
               └────────┬────────┘
                        │
                  Success?
                        │
                       YES
                        │
                        ▼
               ┌─────────────────┐
               │ livenessProbe    │
               │                 │
               │ "Is it alive?"   │
               └────────┬────────┘
                        │
                ┌───────┴────────┐
                │                │
              Healthy          Failed
                │                │
                ▼                ▼
             Continue         Restart
                                container


Meanwhile:

               ┌─────────────────┐
               │ readinessProbe   │
               │                 │
               │ "Can it receive  │
               │  traffic?"       │
               └────────┬────────┘
                        │
                 ┌──────┴──────┐
                 │             │
               Ready        Not Ready
                 │             │
                 ▼             ▼
             Receive       No Service
             traffic        traffic



If interviewer asks:

"What is livenessProbe?"

Say:
"livenessProbe checks whether the application inside a container is still functioning.
If it continuously fails, Kubernetes considers the container unhealthy and restarts the container."

"What is readinessProbe?"
"readinessProbe checks whether a Pod is ready to receive traffic.
If it fails, Kubernetes removes that Pod from the Service's ready endpoints, but it does not restart the container."

"What is startupProbe?"
"startupProbe is used for slow-starting applications. Kubernetes waits for the startup probe to succeed before applying 
liveness and readiness probes. This prevents a slow application from being restarted before it has finished starting."
